<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dust Bowl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dust Bowl was a period of severe dust storms and drought from 1930 to 1940 that badly damaged the farming and ecology of the High Plains and North American prairies, above all western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> panhandles, southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. It was caused by drought, which came in three waves in 1934, 1936 and 1939–1940, and by settlers who had destroyed the topsoil without using dryland farming methods. On &quot;Black Sunday&quot;, 14 April 1935, black blizzards swept the plains. About 3.5 million people left the Plains states, many of them, called Okies, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. John Steinbeck, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie">Woody Guthrie</a> and Dorothea Lange depicted its migrants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dust-bowl.jpg" alt="Dust Bowl" width="330" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur Rothstein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Dust Bowl area lies mainly west of the 100th meridian on the High Plains. It is semiarid, with less than 20 inches of rain a year, and is prone to long droughts and high winds. The Homestead Act of 1862 offered settlers 160 acres, and acreage under cultivation grew after the Civil War and the first transcontinental railroad. An unusually wet period led settlers to believe that rain follows the plow.</p><p>Mechanised plowing and harvesting allowed larger holdings, and demand during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> raised crop prices. On the Llano Estacado the area of farmland doubled between 1900 and 1920 and tripled between 1925 and 1930. Deep plowing displaced the native deep-rooted grasses that held soil and moisture, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> farmers left fields bare in winter and burned stubble. When the drought arrived, the unanchored soil turned to dust.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>An unusually dry era began in the summer of 1930. The first recorded dust storm came on 14 September 1930, and unlike a sand storm its cloud was black or gray and rolled across the ground. On 14 April 1935, Black Sunday, a cold front from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> raised dust in the Dakotas and moved south across the plains at 60 mph. The Associated Press reporter Robert E. Geiger witnessed it, and his editor Edward Stanley coined the term Dust Bowl in rewriting Geiger's story.</p><p>The drought and erosion affected 100 million acres centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> panhandles, with losses reaching $25 million a day by 1936. More than 500,000 Americans were left homeless. About 3.5 million people moved out of the Plains states between 1930 and 1940, many of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, though most migrants moved within their own states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Roosevelt administration established the Soil Erosion Service under Hugh Hammond Bennett in August 1933, which became the Soil Conservation Service in 1935, and the Civilian Conservation Corps planted the Great Plains Shelterbelt of more than 200 million trees. By 1938 the conservation effort had cut the amount of blowing soil by 65 per cent. The drought ended in the fall of 1939 when regular rainfall returned.</p><p>The Farm Security Administration hired photographers such as Dorothea Lange, whose photograph of Florence Owens Thompson became an image of the crisis. John Steinbeck wrote The Grapes of Wrath in 1939, drawing on field notes by Sanora Babb, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie">Woody Guthrie</a>'s Dust Bowl Ballads appeared in 1940. Migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> carried country music to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, a blend later called the Bakersfield Sound.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dust Bowl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
